  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Given name then family name. Family names only date from the 1934 Surname Law, so many are plain Turkish words: Yılmaz ('never gives up'), Demir ('iron'), Kaya ('rock'), Yıldız ('star'). Surnames are the same for men and women; married women usually take the husband's name and may keep their own before it. Lots of people have two given names (Mehmet Emre, Ayşe Nur) and go by one. Kurdish families mostly carry registered Turkish-style surnames, while Kurdish given names such as Baran, Rojda and Berfin have become common, and letters like w and x that were once refused in names are now accepted. Armenian Istanbullus often have surnames ending in -yan built on a trade (Kuyumcuyan, 'jeweller'). Arabic-speaking residents, many from Syria, keep Arabic names. People use first names with a title of respect: Ayşe Hanım, Mehmet Bey, or abi and abla for someone a bit older.",
